      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let's focus on the math, shall we? \n\nThe amortization schedule for your student loan is typically calculated using a formula that takes into account the interest rate, principal balance, and monthly payment. The formula is:\n\nM = P[r(1+r)^n]/[(1+r)^n – 1]\n\nWhere:\nM = monthly payment\nP = principal balance\nr = monthly interest rate (annual rate divided by 12)\nn = number of payments\n\nHowever, this formula assumes that the interest is calculated and added to the principal balance at the beginning of each month. In reality, the interest is usually calculated daily, and it's added to the principal balance at the end of the month.\n\nTo simplify the calculation, let's assume that the interest is calculated daily and added to the principal balance at the end of the month. We can use a formula like this:\n\nP_new = P + (P * r * t)\n\nWhere:\nP_new = new principal balance\nP = original principal balance\nr = monthly interest rate\nt = number of days in the month\n\nNow, let's say your loan has a 6% interest rate, and you make a",
